{"method":"item/completed","params":{"item":{"type":"agentMessage","id":"msg_04aede0663d9ac03016abdb7def188819282247f90d6e3a3d2","text":"TCP congestion control regulates how much data a sender puts into the network to avoid overwhelming it. It uses a congestion window, which limits the amount of unacknowledged data in transit. At the start, TCP increases this window rapidly (slow start), then grows it more gradually as acknowledgments arrive.\n\nWhen packets are lost or delays increase, TCP treats this as congestion and reduces the congestion window. It then cautiously increases the rate again, using mechanisms such as congestion avoidance and fast retransmit/recovery. This feedback loop lets TCP adapt to changing network capacity while sharing bandwidth fairly among connections.","phase":"final_answer","memoryCitation":null,"delivery":null,"questions":null},"threadId":"01a0f516-21c4-7180-b0ab-38289c13eb16","turnId":"01a0f516-2212-7f00-8ad1-bdd805692aeb","completedAtMs":1790818271894},"emittedAtMs":1790818271899}
